A release, a registry and GitHub's answers, with real git, tar and opam:

  $ export GIT_AUTHOR_NAME=t GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL=t@t GIT_COMMITTER_NAME=t GIT_COMMITTER_EMAIL=t@t
  $ export FAKE="$PWD/fake"
  $ mkdir -p "$FAKE/bin"
  $ cp fake_gh.sh "$FAKE/bin/gh" && chmod +x "$FAKE/bin/gh"
  $ export PATH="$FAKE/bin:$PATH"

  $ git init -q -b main "$FAKE/foo" && cd "$FAKE/foo"
  $ mkdir src test
  $ cat > foo.opam <<'EOF'
  > # This file is generated by dune, edit dune-project instead
  > opam-version: "2.0"
  > synopsis: "A scratch package"
  > description: "A scratch package to publish."
  > maintainer: "x <x@example.com>"
  > authors: "x"
  > license: "MIT"
  > homepage: "https://github.com/o/foo"
  > bug-reports: "https://github.com/o/foo/issues"
  > dev-repo: "git+https://github.com/o/foo.git"
  > depends: ["dune" {>= "3.18"}]
  > build: ["dune" "build" "-p" name "-j" jobs]
  > x-open-opam: true
  > EOF
  $ touch dune-project src/foo.mli
  $ echo '# foo' > README.md
  $ echo '(test (name t))' > test/dune
  $ git add . && git commit -qm release && git tag 1.0.0
  $ git archive --format=tar --prefix=foo-1.0.0/ 1.0.0 | gzip -n > "$FAKE/asset.tar.gz"
  $ git rev-parse HEAD > "$FAKE/commit"
  $ cd - > /dev/null

  $ git init -q -b main "$FAKE/registry" && cd "$FAKE/registry"
  $ echo 'opam-version: "2.0"' > repo
  $ git add . && git commit -qm start
  $ git config uploadpack.allowFilter true
  $ cd - > /dev/null
  $ git init -q --bare "$FAKE/fork.git"

  $ sha256() { openssl dgst -sha256 -r "$1" | cut -d' ' -f1; }
  $ SHA=$(sha256 "$FAKE/asset.tar.gz")
  $ COMMIT=$(cat "$FAKE/commit")
  $ release() { # draft immutable asset-digest
  >   cat > "$FAKE/release.json" <<EOF
  > {"tag_name":"1.0.0","draft":$1,"immutable":$2,"published_at":"2026-11-06T12:00:00Z","body":"Fixes the frobnicator.",
  >  "assets":[{"name":"foo-1.0.0.tar.gz","digest":"sha256:$3","browser_download_url":"x"}]}
  > EOF
  > }
  $ attest() { # repository-id commit sha256
  >   cat > "$FAKE/verify.json" <<EOF
  > {"verificationResult":{"statement":{
  >   "subject":[{"digest":{"sha1":"$2"}},{"name":"foo-1.0.0.tar.gz","digest":{"sha256":"$3"}}],
  >   "predicate":{"repositoryId":"$1"}}}}
  > EOF
  > }
  $ green() { echo '[{"check_runs":[{"name":"test","status":"completed","conclusion":"success"}]}]' > "$FAKE/checks.json"; }
  $ release false true "$SHA"; attest 42 "$COMMIT" "$SHA"; green

Every gate that can run here passes, and --dry-run opens nothing:

  $ open-opam publish --repo o/foo 1.0.0 --dry-run
  ✓ Release proof
  ✓ Archive equals tag
  ✓ Exact match
  ✓ Name ownership
  ✓ Metadata and standard
  ✓ Author's CI
  $ test -f "$FAKE/pr" || echo 'no pull request'
  no pull request

Publishing opens one pull request, on a branch of the author's fork. The fork
is behind the registry, whose newer commit changes a workflow, which GitHub
refuses in a push without the token's workflow scope; so the branch starts from
what the fork and the registry share, and carries the release's files alone:

  $ git -C "$FAKE/registry" push -q "$FAKE/fork.git" main
  $ SHARED=$(git -C "$FAKE/registry" rev-parse main)
  $ mkdir -p "$FAKE/registry/.github/workflows" && echo 'on: push' > "$FAKE/registry/.github/workflows/validate.yml"
  $ git -C "$FAKE/registry" add . && git -C "$FAKE/registry" commit -qm 'a workflow'
  $ open-opam publish --repo o/foo 1.0.0 | tail -2
    https://github.com/open-opam/opam-repository/pull/1
  🎉 Done! foo 1.0.0 merges once its checks pass
  $ test "$(git -C "$FAKE/fork.git" rev-parse publish/foo-1.0.0^)" = "$SHARED" && echo 'on the shared commit'
  on the shared commit
  $ grep -c -- '--title Publish foo 1.0.0' "$FAKE/pr"
  1
  $ git -C "$FAKE/fork.git" show --stat --format=%s --name-only publish/foo-1.0.0
  Publish foo 1.0.0
  
  .open-opam/owners/foo.json
  packages/foo/foo.1.0.0/opam

The pull request says what the release is, from its opam file and its notes, then how to check it:

  $ cat "$FAKE/pr_title"
  Publish foo 1.0.0
  $ sed "s/$COMMIT/COMMIT/; s/$SHA/SHA/" "$FAKE/pr_body"
  **foo 1.0.0**: A scratch package
  
  Fixes the frobnicator.
  
  Release: https://github.com/o/foo/releases/tag/1.0.0
  Commit: `COMMIT`
  Asset: `foo-1.0.0.tar.gz`
  SHA-256: `SHA`

Publishing again while its pull request is open finds it, and pushes nothing:

  $ BRANCH=$(git -C "$FAKE/fork.git" rev-parse publish/foo-1.0.0)
  $ open-opam publish --repo o/foo 1.0.0 | tail -3
  ✓ Its pull request is already open
    https://github.com/open-opam/opam-repository/pull/1
  🎉 Done! foo 1.0.0 merges once its checks pass
  $ test "$(git -C "$FAKE/fork.git" rev-parse publish/foo-1.0.0)" = "$BRANCH" && echo 'branch untouched'
  branch untouched

A branch left on the fork with no pull request open, as after one is closed, is replaced:

  $ rm "$FAKE/open_pr" "$FAKE/pr"
  $ open-opam publish --repo o/foo 1.0.0 | tail -2
    https://github.com/open-opam/opam-repository/pull/1
  🎉 Done! foo 1.0.0 merges once its checks pass
  $ test -f "$FAKE/pr" && echo 'opened again'
  opened again

A tool that fails says which step, and what it said, without its hints:

  $ rm "$FAKE/open_pr"; touch "$FAKE/pr_refused"
  $ open-opam publish --repo o/foo 1.0.0 > out 2> err
  [2]
  $ tail -1 out
  ✗ Pull request on open-opam/opam-repository
  $ cat err
    gh: pull request create failed: GraphQL: No commits between main and publish
    Try again in a minute. If it still fails, sign in again with gh auth login.
  $ rm "$FAKE/pr_refused"

The file holds the source without dune's header, then what only the registry writes:

  $ git -C "$FAKE/fork.git" show publish/foo-1.0.0:packages/foo/foo.1.0.0/opam | grep -c 'generated by dune'
  0
  [1]
  $ git -C "$FAKE/fork.git" show publish/foo-1.0.0:packages/foo/foo.1.0.0/opam | tail -5 | sed "s/$COMMIT/COMMIT/; s/$SHA/SHA/"
  x-commit-hash: "COMMIT"
  url {
    src: "https://github.com/o/foo/releases/download/1.0.0/foo-1.0.0.tar.gz"
    checksum: "sha256=SHA"
  }

CI, given that branch, finds every gate passes, so what publish writes is what CI writes again:

  $ git clone -q "$FAKE/registry" ci && git -C ci fetch -q "$FAKE/fork.git" publish/foo-1.0.0:publish
  $ open-opam check --repo o/foo --tag 1.0.0 --checkout ci --base main --head publish
  ✓ Allowed files
  ✓ Release proof
  ✓ Archive equals tag
  ✓ Exact match
  ✓ Name ownership
  ✓ Metadata and standard
  ✓ Author's CI

Without a tag, check judges the pull request as the registry's CI does, reading
the release from the opam files it adds:

  $ open-opam check --checkout ci --base main --head publish
  ✓ Allowed files
  ✓ Release proof
  ✓ Archive equals tag
  ✓ Exact match
  ✓ Name ownership
  ✓ Metadata and standard
  ✓ Author's CI

An account the registry blocked, for a package revoked as malicious, publishes
nothing more, from any of its repositories:

  $ git -C ci switch -q -c blocked main
  $ mkdir -p ci/.open-opam/blocked
  $ cat > ci/.open-opam/blocked/420.json <<'EOF'
  > {"account":"o","actor":"maintainer","evidence":"https://example.com/advisory","blocked_at":"2026-11-13T00:00:00Z"}
  > EOF
  $ git -C ci add . && git -C ci commit -qm block
  $ open-opam check --checkout ci --base blocked --head publish | grep -A1 'Name ownership'
  ✗ Name ownership
    o is blocked here, since a package of theirs was revoked as malicious. If that is a mistake, open an issue on open-opam/opam-repository. [blocked]
  $ git -C ci switch -q main

A change adding a file with no release behind it stops at that:

  $ git -C ci switch -q -c no-release main
  $ mkdir -p ci/packages/bar/bar.1.0.0 && echo 'opam-version: "2.0"' > ci/packages/bar/bar.1.0.0/opam
  $ git -C ci add . && git -C ci commit -qm bar
  $ open-opam check --checkout ci --base main --head no-release
  ✓ Allowed files
  ✗ Release proof
    The release named by an added opam file was not found on GitHub. [not_found]
  [1]
  $ git -C ci switch -q main

An opam file the release does not generate is refused, though it names the
release, so a release publishes no name but its own:

  $ git -C ci switch -q -c extra publish
  $ mkdir -p ci/packages/dune/dune.1.0.0
  $ cat > ci/packages/dune/dune.1.0.0/opam <<EOF
  > opam-version: "2.0"
  > build: ["sh" "-c" "curl evil"]
  > url {
  >   src: "https://github.com/o/foo/releases/download/1.0.0/foo-1.0.0.tar.gz"
  >   checksum: "sha256=$SHA"
  > }
  > EOF
  $ git -C ci add . && git -C ci commit -qm extra
  $ open-opam check --checkout ci --base main --head extra | grep -A1 'Exact match'
  ✗ Exact match
    packages/dune/dune.1.0.0/opam: the release does not generate it. [not_from_the_release]
  $ git -C ci switch -q publish

A forged definition: the real release, and a build command added by hand:

  $ git -C ci switch -q publish
  $ echo 'install: ["curl" "evil"]' >> ci/packages/foo/foo.1.0.0/opam
  $ git -C ci commit -qam forge
  $ open-opam check --repo o/foo --tag 1.0.0 --checkout ci --base main --head publish | grep -A1 'Exact match'
  ✗ Exact match
    packages/foo/foo.1.0.0/opam: differs from what the release generates, first at line 18. [not_generated]

Publishing again finds nothing to do, since the registry already holds these bytes:

  $ git -C "$FAKE/registry" fetch -q "$FAKE/fork.git" publish/foo-1.0.0:publish
  $ git -C "$FAKE/registry" merge -q --squash publish > /dev/null 2>&1 && git -C "$FAKE/registry" commit -qm 'Publish foo 1.0.0'
  $ open-opam publish --repo o/foo 1.0.0 | tail -1
  🎉 Done! foo 1.0.0 is already published

and a version the registry holds with other bytes is never changed:

  $ echo '# changed' >> "$FAKE/registry/packages/foo/foo.1.0.0/opam" && git -C "$FAKE/registry" commit -qam changed
  $ open-opam publish --repo o/foo 1.0.0 | tail -4
  ✓ Registry open-opam/opam-repository
  ✗ Publish 1.0.0
    packages/foo/foo.1.0.0/opam is already published with other bytes. A published version never changes.
  → Release a new version: open-opam release <version>
  $ git -C "$FAKE/registry" reset -q --hard HEAD~1

A release that is not immutable, or still a draft:

  $ release true false "$SHA"
  $ open-opam publish --repo o/foo 1.0.0 --dry-run | head -3
  ✗ Release proof
    The release is still a draft: publish it. [draft]
    The release is not immutable: turn on immutable releases, then make a new release with its archive attached before it is published. [not_immutable]
  $ release false true "$SHA"

An attestation for another repository, or one that fails:

  $ attest 7 "$COMMIT" "$SHA"
  $ open-opam publish --repo o/foo 1.0.0 --dry-run | sed -n 2p
    The attestation names repository 7, and this is 42. [attested_repository]
  $ rm "$FAKE/verify.json"
  $ open-opam publish --repo o/foo 1.0.0 --dry-run | sed -n 2p
    gh release verify refused the release. [not_verified]
  $ attest 42 "$COMMIT" "$SHA"
  $ touch "$FAKE/verify_refused" "$FAKE/asset_refused"
  $ open-opam publish --repo o/foo 1.0.0 --dry-run | sed -n 2,3p
    gh release verify refused the release. [not_verified]
    gh release verify-asset refused the archive. [asset_not_verified]
  $ rm "$FAKE/verify_refused" "$FAKE/asset_refused"

GitHub out of reach is a run that could not finish, never a refused release,
since only a refusal tells CI the release is wrong:

  $ touch "$FAKE/github_down"
  $ open-opam publish --repo o/foo 1.0.0 --dry-run
  ✗ Release proof
    gh: Bad Gateway (HTTP 502)
    Try again in a minute. If it still fails, sign in again with gh auth login.
  [2]
  $ rm "$FAKE/github_down" && touch "$FAKE/verify_down"
  $ open-opam publish --repo o/foo 1.0.0 --dry-run
  ✗ Release proof
    gh: Get "https://api.github.com/repos/o/foo/git/ref/tags%2F1.0.0": dial tcp: lookup api.github.com: no such host
    Try again in a minute. If it still fails, sign in again with gh auth login.
  [2]
  $ rm "$FAKE/verify_down"

A red job, or one still running, and no CI at all:

  $ echo '[{"check_runs":[{"name":"test (5.5)","status":"completed","conclusion":"failure"}]}]' > "$FAKE/checks.json"
  $ open-opam publish --repo o/foo 1.0.0 --dry-run | grep -A1 "Author's CI"
  ✗ Author's CI
    test (5.5) finished as failure. [failed]
  $ echo '[{"check_runs":[{"name":"test","status":"in_progress","conclusion":null}]}]' > "$FAKE/checks.json"
  $ open-opam publish --repo o/foo 1.0.0 --dry-run | grep -A1 "Author's CI" | tail -1
    test is still running: run this again once it has finished. [still_running]
  $ echo '[{"check_runs":[]}]' > "$FAKE/checks.json"
  $ open-opam publish --repo o/foo 1.0.0 --dry-run | grep -A1 "Author's CI" | tail -1
    This commit has no CI result: run CI on pushes to the branch you release from. [no_checks]
  $ green

An archive holding what the tag does not: the xz case.

  $ mkdir hidden && tar -xzf "$FAKE/asset.tar.gz" -C hidden
  $ echo 'backdoor' > hidden/foo-1.0.0/src/extra.ml
  $ cp "$FAKE/asset.tar.gz" clean.tar.gz
  $ tar -czf "$FAKE/asset.tar.gz" -C hidden foo-1.0.0
  $ BAD=$(sha256 "$FAKE/asset.tar.gz")
  $ release false true "$BAD"; attest 42 "$COMMIT" "$BAD"
  $ open-opam publish --repo o/foo 1.0.0 --dry-run | grep -A1 'Archive equals tag'
  ✗ Archive equals tag
    The archive's files are not the tag's: Only in archive/foo-1.0.0/src: extra.ml [archive_differs]
  $ cp clean.tar.gz "$FAKE/asset.tar.gz"; release false true "$SHA"; attest 42 "$COMMIT" "$SHA"

A release nobody offered: a tag whose opam file has no x-open-opam.

  $ cd "$FAKE/foo" && sed -i.bak '/x-open-opam/d' foo.opam && rm foo.opam.bak
  $ git add . && git commit -qm 'no consent' && git tag 1.0.1
  $ git archive --format=tar --prefix=foo-1.0.0/ 1.0.1 | gzip -n > "$FAKE/asset.tar.gz"
  $ git rev-parse HEAD > "$FAKE/commit"
  $ cd - > /dev/null
  $ SHA=$(sha256 "$FAKE/asset.tar.gz"); COMMIT=$(cat "$FAKE/commit")
  $ release false true "$SHA"; attest 42 "$COMMIT" "$SHA"
  $ git -C "$FAKE/foo" tag -f 1.0.0 > /dev/null
  $ open-opam publish --repo o/foo 1.0.0 --dry-run | grep -A1 'Exact match'
  ✗ Exact match
    foo.opam: needs x-open-opam: true, which is how a repository offers a release here: run open-opam init. [no_consent]

The name check. Official opam holds a name from another repository:

  $ official() { # the dev-repo official opam's latest version names
  >   rm -rf "$FAKE/official"; mkdir -p "$FAKE/official/foo/foo.0.9" "$FAKE/official/foo/foo.0.10"
  >   echo 'dev-repo: "git+https://github.com/somewhere/else.git"' | sed '1i\
  > opam-version: "2.0"' > "$FAKE/official/foo/foo.0.9/opam"
  >   printf 'opam-version: "2.0"\ndev-repo: "git+https://%s.git"\n' "$1" > "$FAKE/official/foo/foo.0.10/opam"
  > }
  $ owner() { # the registry's record for foo, by repository number and status
  >   mkdir -p "$FAKE/registry/.open-opam/owners"
  >   printf '{"github_repository_id":%s,"repository":"%s","status":"%s","claimed_at":"2026-01-01T00:00:00Z"}' "$1" "$2" "$3" > "$FAKE/registry/.open-opam/owners/foo.json"
  > }
  $ check_name() { open-opam publish --repo o/foo 1.0.0 --dry-run | grep -A1 'Name ownership'; }
  $ official github.com/real/foo
  $ check_name
  ✗ Name ownership
    foo: official opam's foo comes from real/foo. A fork publishes under its own name, or a consumer uses opam pin. [official_elsewhere]

It reads the latest official version, so 0.10 counts, not 0.9, and its repository may publish:

  $ official github.com/o/foo
  $ check_name | head -1
  ✓ Name ownership

A name official opam points at something we cannot trust:

  $ official gitlab.com/o/foo
  $ check_name
  ✗ Name ownership
    foo: official opam holds this name, and its dev-repo is not on GitHub. [official_unresolved]
  $ official github.com/gone/foo
  $ check_name
  ✗ Name ownership
    foo: official opam holds this name, and the repository it names, gone/foo, no longer exists. [official_unresolved]
  $ rm -rf "$FAKE/official"

A first-come name already taken, by number, whatever the repository is called now:

  $ owner 7 other/foo active
  $ check_name
  ✗ Name ownership
    foo: this name belongs to other/foo. [owned_by_another]

The same number under another address is the same owner, which is the point of it:

  $ owner 42 o/old-name active
  $ check_name | head -1
  ✓ Name ownership

The same address under a new number is someone else, which is also the point:

  $ owner 555 o/foo active
  $ check_name
  ✗ Name ownership
    foo: this name belongs to o/foo. [owned_by_another]

A disputed name, even for its own repository:

  $ owner 42 o/foo disputed
  $ check_name
  ✗ Name ownership
    foo: this name is disputed, so nothing new can publish under it. [disputed]

A fork never takes the name its original publishes, so the original's author
can always claim it:

  $ rm "$FAKE/registry/.open-opam/owners/foo.json" && echo up/foo > "$FAKE/fork_of"
  $ check_name
  ✗ Name ownership
    foo: this repository is a fork of up/foo, which publishes foo. A fork publishes under its own name, such as foo-o. [fork_of_the_original]

unless the registry's maintainer gave the name to the fork, as with its
original author's consent:

  $ owner 42 o/foo active
  $ check_name | head -1
  ✓ Name ownership
  $ rm "$FAKE/fork_of" "$FAKE/registry/.open-opam/owners/foo.json"

A tag that cannot name a version runs nothing:

  $ open-opam publish --repo o/foo --dry-run -- --evil
  ✗ Release proof
    --evil cannot name a version. [invalid_tag]
  [1]

Nor does a repository that cannot be one on GitHub:

  $ open-opam publish --repo=-R/foo --dry-run 1.0.0
  ✗ Release proof
    -R/foo cannot name a GitHub repository. [invalid_repository]
  [1]

A release made as dune-release makes it: an annotated tag, which GitHub attests by the tag object, and a .tbz named for the project, of the tag after dune subst:

  $ owner 42 o/foo active; green
  $ cd "$FAKE/foo"
  $ echo 'x-open-opam: true' >> foo.opam
  $ printf '(lang dune 3.18)\n(name foo)\n' > dune-project
  $ echo 'let version = "%%VERSION%%"' > src/version.ml
  $ git add . && git commit -qm 'a version' && git tag -a 1.0.2 -m 1.0.2
  $ git rev-parse HEAD > "$FAKE/commit"
  $ cd - > /dev/null
  $ git clone -q --branch 1.0.2 "file://$FAKE/foo" "$FAKE/foo-1.0.2" 2> /dev/null
  $ (cd "$FAKE/foo-1.0.2" && dune subst && rm -rf .git)
  $ cat "$FAKE/foo-1.0.2/src/version.ml"
  let version = "1.0.2"
  $ tbz() { # the release's asset, and GitHub's answers for it
  >   tar -cjf "$FAKE/asset.tar.gz" -C "$FAKE" foo-1.0.2
  >   TBZ=$(sha256 "$FAKE/asset.tar.gz"); COMMIT=$(cat "$FAKE/commit")
  >   cat > "$FAKE/release.json" <<JSON
  > {"tag_name":"1.0.2","draft":false,"immutable":true,"published_at":"2026-11-08T12:00:00Z",
  >  "assets":[{"name":"foo-1.0.2.tbz","digest":"sha256:$TBZ","browser_download_url":"x"}]}
  > JSON
  >   attest 42 "$(git -C "$FAKE/foo" rev-parse 1.0.2)" "$TBZ"
  > }
  $ tbz
  $ open-opam publish --repo o/foo 1.0.2 --dry-run
  ✓ Release proof
  ✓ Archive equals tag
  ✓ Exact match
  ✓ Name ownership
  ✓ Metadata and standard
  ✓ Author's CI

Anything beyond what dune subst writes is still a difference:

  $ echo 'backdoor' > "$FAKE/foo-1.0.2/src/extra.ml"
  $ tbz
  $ open-opam publish --repo o/foo 1.0.2 --dry-run | grep -A1 'Archive equals tag'
  ✗ Archive equals tag
    The archive's files are not the tag's: Only in archive/foo-1.0.2/src: extra.ml [archive_differs]
  $ rm "$FAKE/foo-1.0.2/src/extra.ml"

A release holds one archive, so two are refused rather than one chosen:

  $ tbz
  $ sed -i.bak 's/"assets":\[/"assets":[{"name":"foo-1.0.2.tar.gz","digest":"sha256:0","browser_download_url":"x"},/' "$FAKE/release.json" && rm "$FAKE/release.json.bak"
  $ open-opam publish --repo o/foo 1.0.2 --dry-run | grep -A1 'Release proof'
  ✗ Release proof
    The release has several archives, foo-1.0.2.tar.gz, foo-1.0.2.tbz: it needs one, so that one is what is published. [several_archives]
